	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44452</id><title>Flooding and Food Security: The Urgent Need for Research</title><updated>2026-10-28T12:01:51-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44452" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/floodsfoodsecurity.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/floodsfoodsecurity.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/floodsfoodsecurity-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/floodsfoodsecurity.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
URBANA, Illinois, US, October 28 (IPS)  - Recently, countries around the world, including the United States of America, celebrated the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.fao.org/world-food-day/en&quot; data-saferedirecturl=&quot;https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.fao.org/world-food-day/en&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793268286107000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2gI9ATLv-pdz-5h6h61Wu7&quot;&gt;United Nations 2026 World Food Day&lt;/a&gt;. This year’s theme, “Innovate Today. Nourish Tomorrow,” highlighted how efforts to foster innovation and forward-thinking solutions can help to ensure that everyone in our world has access to sufficient, diverse, safe, and nutritious foods while safeguarding the planet for future generations.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44452&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Flooding and Food Security: The Urgent Need for Research”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44444</id><title>We Cannot Afford to Manage Chemicals One at a Time</title><updated>2026-10-28T06:09:07-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44444" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Sheila-Aggarwal-Khan-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;
GENEVA, October 28 (IPS)  - Governments today face an extraordinarily difficult set of tasks: cutting emissions, protecting nature, reducing pollution, creating jobs, strengthening economies, and improving people’s health, all while public finances remain under pressure.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44444&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “We Cannot Afford to Manage Chemicals One at a Time”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44445</id><title>The World Is Watching Norway. Will the Country Protect Antarctica?</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:59:08-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44445" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Arnesen-in-Antarctica_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Arnesen-in-Antarctica_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Arnesen-in-Antarctica_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Arnesen-in-Antarctica_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;OSLO, Norway, October 28 (IPS)  - A global boycott of Antarctic krill products is gathering support. As a Norwegian, I want my country – the world’s largest krill-fishing nation – to change course when it comes to protecting Antarctica.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44445&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The World Is Watching Norway. Will the Country Protect Antarctica?”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44446</id><title>Fifty Years at the Table: What Small Nations Need from the Next UN Secretary-General</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:52:40-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44446" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/image0271026___.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/image0271026___.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/image0271026___-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/image0271026___.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
VICTORIA, Seychelles, October 28 (IPS)  - On 21 September 1976, less than three months after independence, Seychelles took its seat in the General Assembly of the United Nations. We became the 145th member of the Organisation. For a young nation of scattered islands in the Indian Ocean, with a small population and no army to speak of, it was far more than a ceremony. It was a promise that, in the family of nations, size would not decide whose voice was heard.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44446&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Fifty Years at the Table: What Small Nations Need from the Next UN Secretary-General”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44460</id><title>Civilians in Sudan’s El Obeid trapped between drones and incarceration</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44460" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-02-2026-UNICEF-Sudan-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-02-2026-UNICEF-Sudan-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-02-2026-UNICEF-Sudan-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/13-02-2026-UNICEF-Sudan-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Civilians in Sudan’s El Obeid live in constant fear of drone attacks by the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) and of a wave of arbitrary arrests by the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF), UN-appointed independent investigators said on Wednesday. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44460&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Civilians in Sudan’s El Obeid trapped between drones and incarceration”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44459</id><title>UN human rights chief urges better protection of civilians in conflicts</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44459" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/30-07-2026_UNICEF_Ukraine.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/30-07-2026_UNICEF_Ukraine.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/30-07-2026_UNICEF_Ukraine.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/30-07-2026_UNICEF_Ukraine.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Youth-led protests against corruption and inequality in Serbia, Bangladesh, Nepal and Madagascar reflect a growing demand for reinforcing human rights around the world, UN human rights chief Volker Türk said at a press briefing on Wednesday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44459&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “UN human rights chief urges better protection of civilians in conflicts”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44458</id><title>Backing for UN call to end Cuba embargo falls to 20-year low</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44458" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Graphics%20Library/2026/10/28-10-2026-UN-Photo-GA-vote-Cuba.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Graphics%20Library/2026/10/28-10-2026-UN-Photo-GA-vote-Cuba.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Graphics%20Library/2026/10/28-10-2026-UN-Photo-GA-vote-Cuba.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Graphics%20Library/2026/10/28-10-2026-UN-Photo-GA-vote-Cuba.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN General Assembly on Wednesday called for the 34th time for an end to the US economic, commercial and financial embargo on Cuba – but with the weakest backing for the resolution in two decades.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44458&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Backing for UN call to end Cuba embargo falls to 20-year low”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44457</id><title>Ending conflict is ‘number one thing’ to reduce world hunger, new WFP chief says</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44457" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/21-05-2026_WFP_Haiti-10.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/21-05-2026_WFP_Haiti-10.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/21-05-2026_WFP_Haiti-10.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/21-05-2026_WFP_Haiti-10.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Reducing conflict is the single most effective way to tackle global hunger, the new head of the UN World Food Programme (WFP), Luke Lindberg, said on Wednesday, noting that conflict drives two thirds of the acute food insecurity affecting 266 million people worldwide.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44457&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Ending conflict is ‘number one thing’ to reduce world hunger, new WFP chief says”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44456</id><title>Protecting the storytellers: Women journalists on the frontline of conflict</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44456" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/13-08-2024-UN-Women-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/13-08-2024-UN-Women-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/13-08-2024-UN-Women-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/13-08-2024-UN-Women-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As online abuse, gender-based attacks and impunity ramp up the threats to women reporting from crisis zones, journalists and UN leaders are calling for urgent action to protect those who document conflict. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44456&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Protecting the storytellers: Women journalists on the frontline of conflict”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44455</id><title>DR Congo: WHO urges sustained efforts as Ebola cases top 10,000</title><updated>2026-10-28T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44455" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-WHO-DRC-ebola.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-WHO-DRC-ebola.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-WHO-DRC-ebola.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-WHO-DRC-ebola.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As Ebola cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) surpass the 10,000 mark, the World Health Organization (WHO) has underlined the need for continued efforts to contain spread of the disease. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/28/44455&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “DR Congo: WHO urges sustained efforts as Ebola cases top 10,000”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0096s ch:0 -->